      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Progressive decrease in packed cell volume (PCV), red blood cell (RBC) count, and hemoglobin, and hypoplasia of erythroid elements of bone marrow are predictable features of chronic kidney disease (CKD).",
              "pdf_page": 162,
              "printed_page": 80,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Anemia is normocytic, normochromic, nonregenerative, and proportional to stage of CKD; underlying cause of anemia is multifactorial.",
              "pdf_page": 162,
              "printed_page": 80,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Gastrointestinal (GI) blood loss, reduced RBC survival, deficiencies in iron and/or folate, cytokines, and inflammatory mediators may be involved; however, primary cause of anemia in CKD is inadequate production of erythropoietin (EPO) by the kidneys.",
              "pdf_page": 162,
              "printed_page": 80,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "EPO is hormone that regulates bone marrow RBC generation and is produced in peritubular interstitial cells in response to decreased tissue oxygen.",
              "pdf_page": 162,
              "printed_page": 80,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
